       The  tteemmppnnaamm()  function  returns a pointer to a string that is a valid
       filename, and such that a file with this name did not exist when  tteemmpp‐‐
       nnaamm()  checked.   The  filename  suffix  of the pathname generated will
       start with _p_f_x in case _p_f_x is a non‐NULL string of at most five  bytes.
       The  directory  prefix part of the pathname generated is required to be
       "appropriate" (often that at least implies writable).

       Attempts to find an appropriate  directory  go  through  the  following
       steps:

       (1)  In  case  the  environment variable TTMMPPDDIIRR exists and contains the
            name of an appropriate directory, that is used.

       (2)  Otherwise, if the _d_i_r argument is non‐NULL and appropriate, it  is
            used.

