NNOOTTEESS
       Although tteemmppnnaamm() generates names that are difficult to guess, it is
       nevertheless possible that between the time that tteemmppnnaamm() returns a
       pathname, and the time that the program opens it, another program might
       create that pathname using ooppeenn(2), or create it as a symbolic link.
       This can lead to security holes.  To avoid such possibilities, use the
       ooppeenn(2) OO__EEXXCCLL flag to open the pathname.  Or better yet, use
       mmkksstteemmpp(3) or ttmmppffiillee(3).

       SUSv2 does not mention the use of TTMMPPDDIIRR; glibc will use it only when
       the program is not set‐user‐ID.  On SVr4, the directory used under ((44))
       is _/_t_m_p (and this is what glibc does).

       Because it dynamically allocates memory used to return the pathname,
       tteemmppnnaamm() is reentrant, and thus thread safe, unlike ttmmppnnaamm(3).

       The tteemmppnnaamm() function generates a different string each time it is
       called, up to TTMMPP__MMAAXX (defined in _<_s_t_d_i_o_._h_>) times.  If it is called
       more than TTMMPP__MMAAXX times, the behavior is implementation defined.

       tteemmppnnaamm() uses at most the first five bytes from _p_f_x.

       The glibc implementation of tteemmppnnaamm() fails with the error EEEEXXIISSTT upon
       failure to find a unique name.

BBUUGGSS
       The precise meaning of "appropriate" is undefined; it is unspecified
       how accessibility of a directory is determined.

